## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that a UK judge, Sir Ross Cranston of the High Court, did issue a ruling in 2019 regarding the P&ID case, and this ruling was subsequently reported on by the Daily Trust in September 2020. The evidence, including court documents cited in news reports, clearly states that the judge found Vice President Yemi Osinbajo's interventions and a 2018 handwritten note labeling the deal "a fraud on the nation" were "instrumental" in uncovering the fraud and helped turn the case in Nigeria's favor.

Given that the evidence is consistent and authoritative, no further information is required.

The claim that the Daily Trust news report is "fake news" is directly contradicted by the available evidence. The report accurately reflects the content of a genuine UK court ruling. Therefore, the claim is false.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check establishes that a UK judge, Sir Ross Cranston, issued a [ruling in the P&ID case](https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2019/9/26/uk-judge-allows-nigeria-to-appeal-9bn-asset-forfeiture-ruling) in 2019, which was [reported on by the Daily Trust](https://dailytrust.com/how-osinbajo-detected-pid-fraud-against-nigeria-uk-judge/) in September 2020. The [judge's ruling](https://www.chronicle.ng/2020/09/osinbajo-detected-pid-fraud-nigeria-judge/), as documented in court records, explicitly credited Vice President Yemi Osinbajo's 2018 intervention and handwritten note calling the deal "a fraud on the nation" as instrumental in uncovering the fraud. Therefore, the claim that the Daily Trust report is "fake news" is false, as the report accurately reflects a genuine court ruling.